# System 3: agentic-search-ui configuration
#
# Copy to .env and fill in real values: `cp env.example .env`
# .env is gitignored. Never commit real secrets. Dev and prod use separate credentials.
# This is a placeholder template only. Values below are examples or empty.
# The env surface is derived from the Phase 4.0 capability sheet
# (requirements/phase_4/API_capability_sheet.md) and the locked stack decisions.
# It is refined during the Phase 6 build.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Layer 1: knowledge graph connection (Hetzner AGE graph, READ-ONLY)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TWO transports exist and exactly one is used per process. `execute_cypher`
# dispatches on GRAPH_QUERY_URL: set it and every Layer 1 call goes over HTTPS,
# leave it empty and every call goes over psycopg2. Passing a connection_factory
# while it is set raises rather than silently picking one, so a misconfiguration
# is loud instead of invisible. Settled at build phase 4.11.
#
# Transport 1, HTTPS, the v1 transport (Decision D, Section 24). LIVE since
# 2026-08-22 on the Hetzner box, behind an automatic-HTTPS reverse proxy, on the
# read-only kg_reader role, re-running this repository's own Cypher validator
# server-side so a bug in the client-side validator is not the only barrier.
# Prefer this. It needs no SSH session, which is the whole reason it was built:
# the hand-opened tunnel below went down twice in one week and forced build
# phase 4.6 to merge with three live checks unrun.
# Operations, including how to rotate the credential: see
# docs/data-engineering/Graph_query_service_runbook.md
GRAPH_QUERY_URL=
# Bearer credential for that service. Required alongside GRAPH_QUERY_URL; the
# service authenticates every call against it, never an IP allowlist alone,
# because Railway does not guarantee a static egress IP. Its real value is
# generated ON the box and never leaves it except into a caller's own .env.
GRAPH_QUERY_TOKEN=
#
# Transport 2, psycopg2 direct. The prototype transport from build phase 2.1,
# still supported and still the rollback path if the service is ever suspect.
# Postgres binds 127.0.0.1 only on the box, so reaching it from anywhere else
# needs a local port-forward opened by hand:
#   ssh -N -L 15432:127.0.0.1:5432 root@46.225.128.133
# then GRAPH_PG_HOST=127.0.0.1 and GRAPH_PG_PORT=15432. The credential MUST be
# read-only by design; kg_reader carries default_transaction_read_only, so this
# is enforcement at the connection level rather than an instruction.
GRAPH_PG_HOST=
GRAPH_PG_PORT=5432
GRAPH_PG_USER=kg_reader
GRAPH_PG_PASSWORD=
GRAPH_PG_DBNAME=ncbi_kg

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LLM harness (three tiers: guard, plan, synth over LiteLLM + OpenRouter)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OpenRouter is the primary gateway. The model per tier is chosen in Phase 6 by
# model-bench, so add only the provider keys the chosen models actually need.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=
# Optional explicit model id per tier (else resolved in app config):
GUARD_MODEL=
PLAN_MODEL=
SYNTH_MODEL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Layer 2: NCBI APIs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NCBI_API_KEY raises E-utilities from 3 to 10 requests/second (free key).
# NCBI_EMAIL is sent as the E-utilities tool and email parameter (etiquette).
# No key is required for Datasets API v2, Variation Services, or PubChem.
NCBI_API_KEY=
NCBI_EMAIL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Layer 3: enrichment APIs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# No key is required for PubTator3, LitVar2, LitSense, or ClinicalTrials.gov v2.
# Documented here so no one hunts for credentials that do not exist.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# User data database (PostgreSQL, separate from the knowledge graph)
# Holds auth users plus the interactions and cq_candidates feedback-loop tables.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
USER_DB_URL=postgresql://localhost:5432/search_agent_users

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cache (Redis) for Layer 2 and Layer 3 API responses
# Reference TTLs: gene 1 week, variant 1 week, publication 1 day.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379/0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Auth
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
AUTH_SECRET=
# Comma-separated user UUIDs allowed to set RequestContext.operator_mode=true
# on POST /query and see real cost/token data instead of the redacted,
# end-user-facing response (Section 19.4). Empty or unset means nobody is
# allowed: operator_mode is silently ignored (fail-closed), never granted by
# default. No role/permission system exists yet (Section 15), so this
# allowlist is the interim access control until one does.
OPERATOR_USER_IDS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Observability
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LangSmith: per-run tracing (Section 20.1). PostHog: behavioral analytics
# (Section 20.2). The tool-call audit log (Section 20.3) needs no credential
# and is on by default, since it implements a PRD audit obligation rather
# than an optional integration.
#
# TRACING NEEDS BOTH THE FLAG AND A KEY. LANGCHAIN_TRACING_V2 below has been
# `true` in this file since long before tracing was built, so on its own it
# carries no operator intent, and the installed langsmith honours that
# spelling. Leaving the key blank is what keeps tracing off, and the code
# requires both rather than the flag alone precisely so that a blank key
# means provably zero outbound calls rather than a stream of failing ones.
LANGSMITH_API_KEY=
LANGSMITH_PROJECT=agentic-search-ui
LANGCHAIN_TRACING_V2=true

# PostHog's project key (public, `phc_`-prefixed, write-only) travels in the
# request BODY as `api_key`, not as a header. Blank means analytics are off
# and nothing is sent.
#
# POSTHOG_HOST is the INGEST host, which carries the `.i.` subdomain.
# `https://us.posthog.com` also answers today, and was what this file said
# until build phase 5.0 (F-5.0-06), but the documented capture endpoint is
# the one below and "works today" is not a contract. EU projects use
# `https://eu.i.posthog.com`.
POSTHOG_API_KEY=
POSTHOG_HOST=https://us.i.posthog.com

# The Section 20.3 audit sink. Gitignored via `logs/`. Set an absolute path
# to write outside the working tree, which is what a deployed environment
# should do. TOOL_AUDIT_LOG_ENABLED=false switches it off, and only the
# literal word `false` does so: `0` deliberately leaves auditing ON, since
# guessing wrong in the other direction silently discards an audit trail.
TOOL_AUDIT_LOG_PATH=logs/tool_audit.jsonl
TOOL_AUDIT_LOG_ENABLED=true

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cost control (safety-critical hard caps). Starter values, tune in app config.
# PER_USER_DAILY_QUERY_CAP is a query COUNT (100/day), not a dollar figure;
# the other three caps below are USD or seconds, as their suffix states.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
PER_QUERY_COST_CAP_USD=
PER_USER_DAILY_QUERY_CAP=
SYSTEM_DAILY_CAP_USD=
PER_STEP_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30
# ANON_DAILY_RUN_CAP is a run COUNT: how many runs ALL anonymous callers
# together may start in one UTC day. It is the only bound on TOTAL anonymous
# spend, because the two caps above cannot reach a caller with no account:
# PER_USER_DAILY_QUERY_CAP is keyed on a users row, and SYSTEM_DAILY_CAP_USD
# sums the interactions table, which nothing writes yet. Setting this too
# high is the same as not setting it: build phase 4.10's adversary round
# accepted 40 paid pipelines in 0.25 seconds with no bound in place.
#
# It is NOT the only bound on ONE anonymous caller, and treating it as one
# is what F-4.10-R-01 measured. A per-identity attempt ceiling
# (ATTEMPT_ALLOWANCE in data/guest_sessions.py, deliberately a product
# constant rather than an env var) limits how many runs a single guest can
# start, refused or not. Without it, one guest token drained this whole
# ceiling in 1.68 seconds and took the anonymous product offline for
# everyone else for the rest of the UTC day.
ANON_DAILY_RUN_CAP=200

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# App config
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# APP_ENV names the deployment and is reported by GET /health as `app_env`, so
# anyone holding a URL can tell which environment answered. Build phase 4.15
# set it to `production` and `develop` on the two Railway environments. It is
# not a secret and it must differ between them: the premise gate's P5 arm
# fails when both report the same value.
APP_ENV=development
PORT=8000
# One origin per environment, never a shared list. Section 24 requires a
# separate variable set per environment; build phase 4.15's P4 arm fails if
# either API admits the OTHER environment's web origin.
CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000
LOG_LEVEL=info

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deployment-only (set on the Railway services, not used for local dev)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Added at build phase 4.15 as finding F-4.15-01. All three were already set on
# the deployed services and documented nowhere, so provisioning a second
# environment from this file would have silently omitted them. The first is the
# one that matters.
#
# RUN_MIGRATIONS_ON_STARTUP gates `alembic upgrade head`, the first half of the
# start command in railway.json. Unset or false, a deploy serves the new code
# against the old schema.
RUN_MIGRATIONS_ON_STARTUP=true
# Nixpacks builder hints rather than application config. NO_CACHE forces a
# clean build; NODE_VERSION pins the frontend's toolchain.
NIXPACKS_NO_CACHE=1
NIXPACKS_NODE_VERSION=20

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Frontend (Vite dev server, frontend/src/lib/api.ts)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Absolute backend URL for the frontend's fetch calls. Empty means same-origin
# (the default for local dev when the Vite dev server proxies or the backend
# serves the built frontend). Set only when the frontend and backend run as
# two separate processes on two separate ports, as build phase 1.2's
# Playwright E2E suite does (see frontend/playwright.config.ts).
VITE_API_BASE_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build harness, NOT required to run the product
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing under src/ reads this. It belongs to the build harness's alternate
# metered backend, and it is listed here only so that this file is a complete
# key-for-key record of what a .env on a working machine holds. Everything
# else about that backend, the model identifiers, the prices and where its
# credentials live, stays deliberately out of every tracked file; see
# requirements/phase_6/Continuation_prompt.md under "Which session to open".
# Leave blank unless you are running the alternate backend.
BUILD_OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
